# Proprietary File Headers (Thesium)

Applies to **proprietary Thesium repositories** (`thesium-workflows`,
`thesium-career`, `risk-dashboard`, and any repo whose `LICENSE` file
contains `"Proprietary"` or `"All Rights Reserved"`).

<danger>
  Never stamp an Apache-2.0 copyright block on a proprietary file.
  `project/header-copyright` (810) is for OSS repos only.
</danger>

## Required fields

Every source file opens with a header comment block in the language's
native comment style. All six fields are required:

| Field | Content |
|-------|---------|
| `Title` | Product or repo name (e.g. `Thesium Warning Flags`) |
| `Subtitle` | One-line description of this specific file |
| `Author` | `Christopher Lester` |
| `Line` | `Founder, Miniforge AI` |
| `Copyright` | `2025-2026 Christopher Lester (christopher@miniforge.ai)` |
| Proprietary notice | `All rights reserved. Proprietary and confidential. See LICENSE.` |

## Format

Adapt the comment delimiter to the language; the field order and wording
are fixed.

<example>
Clojure / ClojureScript:

;; Title: Thesium Warning Flags
;; Subtitle: detection-rule registry (data) + per-issuer evaluator
;; Author: Christopher Lester
;; Line: Founder, Miniforge AI
;; Copyright 2025-2026 Christopher Lester (christopher@miniforge.ai).
;; All rights reserved. Proprietary and confidential. See LICENSE.
</example>

<example>
Swift / Rust / Python (line-comment style):

// Title: Thesium Career
// Subtitle: State-machine tests for DashboardViewModel
// Author: Christopher Lester
// Line: Founder, Miniforge AI
// Copyright 2025-2026 Christopher Lester (christopher@miniforge.ai). All rights reserved.
// Proprietary and confidential. See LICENSE.
</example>

<example type="invalid">
;; Copyright 2025 Miniforge AI
;; Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0

Reason: wrong owner name, wrong license — Apache-2.0 must never appear
in a proprietary file.
</example>

## Context

- Apply when creating or editing any source file in a proprietary Thesium repo.
- Do not strip or reformat existing headers — they are required notices, not commentary (see `foundations/self-documenting-code` dewey 009).
- Update the year range when the file is substantially revised in a new calendar year.
